# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about factors influencing agriculture, per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. Rice requires at least 1,520 mm of well-distributed annual rainfall, with the heaviest rain during the growing season and comparatively little during harvesting.\n2. Rubber's latex yield diminishes appreciably above the 760-metre contour, while tea and coffee thrive best on elevated ground.\n3. Sandy soils, with coarse particles and few colloids, are highly moisture-retentive and are best suited to rice cultivation.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect and reverses the actual soil properties — the text describes CLAYEY (not sandy) soils as tightly bound, sticky and highly moisture-retentive, making them well-suited to rice; sandy soils instead allow water/air to pass quickly and suit crops needing aeration, like groundnuts. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Match List-I (Crop/Industry Disaster) with List-II (Cause, per Goh Cheng Leong) and select the correct answer using the code below.\nList-I:\nA. Irish Potato Famine (1845-48)\nB. Destruction of Sea Island Cotton (from 1890s)\nC. Destruction of Sri Lankan coffee (1878)\nD. Californian/Mediterranean vineyard destruction (1870s)\nList-II:\n1. Boll-weevil\n2. Phylloxera insect pest\n3. Phytophthora infestans fungus\n4. Coffee Blight\nCodes:","o":["A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2","A-1, B-3, C-4, D-2","A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2","A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4"],"a":0,"e":"The correct match is A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2: the Irish Potato Famine was caused by the fungus Phytophthora infestans; the boll-weevil destroyed Sea Island Cotton; the Coffee Blights destroyed Sri Lankan coffee (forcing a switch to tea); and Phylloxera destroyed Californian/Mediterranean vineyards."})

# ==================================================================
# SECTION 2: SOIL EROSION AND CONSERVATION
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about soil erosion and conservation, per Goh Cheng Leong, is NOT correct?","o":["Strip-cropping involves planting a single crop uniformly across an entire field to maximise yield, rather than alternating different crops in parallel strips","Contour ploughing, done at right angles to the hill slope, impedes downward water flow and checks soil creep, preventing gully formation","The Norfolk System of crop rotation typically involves potatoes, wheat, a nitrogen-fixing legume, and then a cereal again in succeeding years","Cover-cropping uses fast-growing plants like sweet potatoes or legumes to protect bare soil beneath slow-maturing tree crops such as rubber, oil palm, tea or coffee"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the actual definition — strip-cropping is explicitly the planting of DIFFERENT crops in ALTERNATE strips (ripening/harvested at different times), specifically to avoid leaving the entire field bare at once, not a single uniform crop across the field. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"According to Goh Cheng Leong, what was the Tennessee Valley Authority (T.V.A.), and what did it achieve?","o":["A U.S. multi-purpose dam programme, begun in 1933, that rehabilitated a devastated farming region through irrigation, hydro-electric power, flood control and improved river navigability","A private American agribusiness founded to develop the Cotton Belt's export infrastructure","An international body established to regulate world wheat export quotas among major producers","A soil conservation NGO focused solely on afforestation projects in the south-eastern United States"],"a":0,"e":"The text explicitly describes the T.V.A. as a multi-purpose dam programme (over 30 dams since 1933) on the Tennessee River and its tributaries, rehabilitating a badly devastated 104,000 sq km region through combined irrigation, hydro-electric power generation, flood control and improved navigation — not a private agribusiness, an international quota body, or an NGO limited to afforestation."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about irrigation methods described by Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. Basin irrigation, the traditional method of the Nile valley, retains annual flood water in rectangular basin-like fields for later use.\n2. Inundation canals in the Ganges and Indus basins fill with floodwater during the rainy season but dry up in winter when demand is greatest, making them less satisfactory than perennial canal irrigation.\n3. Tank irrigation involves sinking deep tube wells with electric pumps to draw water from over 90 metres below ground.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the text describes that as WELL irrigation (using tube wells with electric pumps reaching depths of over 92 metres), not tank irrigation, which instead involves small mud-bund reservoirs ('tanks') collecting rainy-season runoff, common in India and Sri Lanka. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

# ==================================================================
# SECTION 3: TYPES OF AGRICULTURE
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Match List-I (Type of Agriculture, per Goh Cheng Leong) with List-II (Defining Characteristic) and select the correct answer using the code below.\nList-I:\nA. Primitive subsistence agriculture\nB. Intensive subsistence agriculture\nC. Plantation agriculture\nD. Extensive mechanized cereal cultivation\nList-II:\n1. Commercialised tropical farming on large estates, initiated by European colonists, solely for export\n2. Wet padi farming with dense population, high yields, but little or no export surplus, typical of China/India/Japan\n3. Shifting cultivation with primitive implements on tropical lowlands/hill slopes, with no surplus\n4. Farms of 240-16,200 hectares in continental climates (Canada, USA, Argentina, Australia) with all stages mechanized\nCodes:","o":["A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4","A-2, B-3, C-1, D-4","A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4","A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1"],"a":0,"e":"The correct match is A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4: primitive subsistence agriculture is shifting cultivation with no surplus; intensive subsistence agriculture is high-yield wet padi farming with no export surplus; plantation agriculture is commercialised colonial-era export farming; and extensive mechanized cereal cultivation is large-scale, fully mechanized New World farming."})

upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about pastoral and mixed farming, per Goh Cheng Leong, is NOT correct?","o":["Transhumance refers exclusively to the seasonal movement of nomadic herders across semi-arid steppes, and is a term never applied to Alpine cattle-driving practices","Transhumance is important in Norway, Switzerland, Austria and the Rocky Mountains, involving driving cattle from valleys to alpine pastures in summer and back in winter","If seasonal animal migration occurs across semi-arid steppes rather than mountain pastures, it is instead called nomadic herding","Mixed farming combines arable farming and animal husbandry, and is widely practised in Britain and many parts of Europe"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the actual usage — the text explicitly applies 'transhumance' specifically to the ALPINE, mountain-valley cattle-driving practice (Norway, Switzerland, Austria, Rockies), while migration across semi-arid STEPPES is instead termed 'nomadic herding', not transhumance. The other three statements are correct."})

# ==================================================================
# SECTION 4: WORLD CROPS
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"According to the crop data presented by Goh Cheng Leong, wet padi yields typically compare to dry padi yields, and rice enters international trade, in which of the following ways?","o":["Wet padi normally yields about double that of dry padi, and only between 3% and 5% of world rice production enters international trade","Wet padi and dry padi yield roughly the same amount, and more than half of world rice production is exported","Dry padi yields substantially more than wet padi, and almost all rice production is traded internationally","Wet padi yields double that of dry padi, and about 50% of world rice production enters international trade"],"a":0,"e":"The text explicitly states wet padi normally doubles the yield of dry padi, and that only between 3% and 5% of rice enters international trade, with the bulk produced and consumed within Asia itself — not the other yield or trade-share combinations."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about wheat, per Goh Cheng Leong's world crop data:\n1. About 80% of the world's wheat is autumn-sown winter wheat, which is hard, palatable and has a high gluten content.\n2. Spring wheat is generally harder and more nutritious than winter wheat, making it preferred for bread-making.\n3. Wheat yields are typically lower on extensive mechanized New World farms than on intensive European farms.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 2 is incorrect and reverses the actual characterisation — the text describes spring wheat as SOFTER and coarser (more suitable for biscuits/cakes/pastes), while winter wheat is the harder, higher-gluten type preferred for bread. Statements 1 and 3 are correct."})

# ==================================================================
# SECTION 5: NCERT — INDIA-SPECIFIC LAND USE, CROPPING SEASONS, GREEN REVOLUTION
# ==================================================================

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about India's land-use categories, per NCERT:\n1. 'Culturable Wasteland' refers to land left fallow for more than five years, which can be reclaimed for cultivation.\n2. 'Current Fallow' refers to land left uncultivated for one year or less, allowing natural replenishment of fertility.\n3. Common Property Resources (CPRs), such as village pasture lands and water bodies, are typically owned by individual private farmers rather than the community.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect — NCERT explicitly defines CPRs as owned by the STATE/community for community use, NOT by individual private farmers; this is precisely why they are distinguished from private land. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about India's cropping seasons, per NCERT — a classification not used by Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. The Kharif season coincides with the South-West Monsoon and supports crops like rice, cotton, jute, jowar and bajra.\n2. The Rabi season begins with the onset of winter (October-November) and suits temperate/subtropical crops like wheat, gram and mustard.\n3. This three-season distinction (kharif, rabi, zaid) applies uniformly across all of India, including the southern peninsula.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect — NCERT explicitly states this three-season distinction does NOT exist in southern India, where uniformly high temperatures allow tropical crops to be grown up to three times a year given sufficient soil moisture, unlike the sharply defined seasons of northern/interior India. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about the Green Revolution in India, per NCERT:\n1. It relied on High-Yielding Varieties (HYVs) of wheat (from Mexico) and rice (from the Philippines), combined with chemical fertilisers, introduced by the mid-1960s.\n2. It was initially confined mainly to irrigated areas such as Punjab, Haryana, Western Uttar Pradesh, Andhra Pradesh and Gujarat, creating regional disparities before spreading further from the 1970s.\n3. It was launched in direct response to a food surplus crisis, when India had excess foodgrain stocks it could not export profitably.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect and reverses the actual cause — the Green Revolution was launched in response to a food SHORTAGE crisis (following two consecutive mid-1960s droughts that forced foodgrain imports), not a surplus. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Assertion (A): Despite the Green Revolution's success, per capita crop yields in India remain lower than in countries like the U.S.A., Russia and Japan.\nReason (R): Very high population pressure on limited land resources in India keeps overall labour productivity in agriculture comparatively low.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above Assertion (A) and Reason (R)?","o":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true and R explains A — NCERT explicitly links India's comparatively low per-hectare crop yields and low agricultural labour productivity to the country's very high population pressure on its limited land resources, a distinct structural constraint separate from technology adoption alone."})

